1
00:00:08,431 --> 00:00:10,890
Now let's get into load balancing.

2
00:00:12,030 --> 00:00:14,030
So what is load balancing?

3
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:19,133
So a load balancer serves as
the point of entry for a service, and

4
00:00:19,133 --> 00:00:24,670
directs traffic to one of N servers
that can handle the request.

5
00:00:24,670 --> 00:00:29,573
It's used both for
scaling and for resilience,

6
00:00:29,573 --> 00:00:32,950
and it likely involves NAT.

7
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:37,320
So just to kind of clarify all that, so
shown here, we're running some service.

8
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:42,052
So let's say we're running some
web application that accepts

9
00:00:42,052 --> 00:00:43,906
individual requests.

10
00:00:43,906 --> 00:00:48,055
And for
scaling purposes we can add multiple

11
00:00:48,055 --> 00:00:52,970
hosts because our services
is pretty popular.

12
00:00:52,970 --> 00:00:57,995
And so now I want to be able for
each given request,

13
00:00:57,995 --> 00:01:03,070
choose a server to handle that request.

14
00:01:03,070 --> 00:01:07,510
And maybe a different request comes in and
goes to a different server.

15
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:13,554
So it's balancing the load
across the service.

16
00:01:15,515 --> 00:01:23,137
So, part of this is going to
include a load balancing algorithm.

17
00:01:23,137 --> 00:01:28,470
So the client's going to initiate
a TCP connection to the service.

18
00:01:28,470 --> 00:01:34,370
And some load balancer must select which
server to forward the request to and

19
00:01:34,370 --> 00:01:40,018
establish the connection other end and
handle the ultimate request.

20
00:01:40,018 --> 00:01:43,676
And this is where a load
balancing algorithm comes in.

21
00:01:43,676 --> 00:01:49,066
And so this is important because you have
a lot of different choices of algorithms,

22
00:01:49,066 --> 00:01:52,540
and you can choose them based
on what fits your needs.

23
00:01:54,430 --> 00:01:59,284
An important consideration
here is the server set.

24
00:01:59,284 --> 00:02:04,324
So that's which servers are part
of the set to choose from.

25
00:02:04,324 --> 00:02:08,317
So the set is typically
assigned statically.

26
00:02:08,317 --> 00:02:15,807
So you just add the servers, but
it may include some liveness checks.

27
00:02:15,807 --> 00:02:19,381
So maybe the load balancer
itself is sending

28
00:02:19,381 --> 00:02:22,840
out heartbeats to say are you still okay?

29
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:23,704
Are you okay?

30
00:02:23,704 --> 00:02:24,796
Are you okay?

31
00:02:24,796 --> 00:02:29,383
And then keeping track
of that in some table,

32
00:02:29,383 --> 00:02:34,693
which is the set of servers
that it can choose among,

33
00:02:34,693 --> 00:02:40,382
and that kind of forms one of
the inputs to the algorithm.

34
00:02:40,382 --> 00:02:45,850
Another important consideration is
something called flow affinity.

35
00:02:46,910 --> 00:02:52,850
And so why this is important
is because TCP is stateful.

36
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:57,749
So, we need to ensure all packets
from the same flow from the same

37
00:02:57,749 --> 00:03:01,210
(TCP connection) go to the same server.

38
00:03:01,210 --> 00:03:06,790
So the first packet,
an algorithm will select the server.

39
00:03:06,790 --> 00:03:13,170
It'll use the IP of the server
is called the VIP or virtual IP.

40
00:03:15,370 --> 00:03:17,561
And so it'll pick the VIP,

41
00:03:17,561 --> 00:03:22,210
and then subsequent packets
all need to look in a table.

42
00:03:22,210 --> 00:03:27,251
So the first packet will come through,
it'll use the algorithm to

43
00:03:27,251 --> 00:03:32,471
select the server, then it'll write
into the table an entry that has

44
00:03:32,471 --> 00:03:37,942
a mapping between the source IP and
port, and the VIP, the virtual IP.

45
00:03:37,942 --> 00:03:40,270
So which backend server it chose.

46
00:03:41,650 --> 00:03:46,488
All right, so why that's important
again is now we have a TCP connection.

47
00:03:46,488 --> 00:03:48,590
So let me flip back to this picture.

48
00:03:50,130 --> 00:03:53,788
So we have client,
it opened a TCP session, and

49
00:03:53,788 --> 00:03:58,862
that TCP session is going to be
terminated on one of these servers.

50
00:03:58,862 --> 00:04:03,121
So it's going to have all those
sequence numbers, the packet buffers,

51
00:04:03,121 --> 00:04:07,826
what's been acknowledged, and that's
going to be all stored on this host.

52
00:04:07,826 --> 00:04:12,674
So the client always must
talk to the same server.

53
00:04:12,674 --> 00:04:15,000
That's what flow affinity means.

54
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:20,038
All right, so
now getting into the algorithm.

55
00:04:20,038 --> 00:04:22,270
So let's go through a couple.

56
00:04:22,270 --> 00:04:25,550
So one would be source hashing.

57
00:04:25,550 --> 00:04:29,332
So for source hashing,
for a source IP and port,

58
00:04:29,332 --> 00:04:34,050
a hash function determines
the back-end server.

59
00:04:34,050 --> 00:04:38,909
So this balances connections among
servers under the assumption

60
00:04:38,909 --> 00:04:43,614
that you have a distribution of
source IP addresses and ports.

61
00:04:43,614 --> 00:04:48,255
So if you have a lot of clients,
chances are they're going to have a nice

62
00:04:48,255 --> 00:04:53,560
distribution of source IP addresses and
port numbers that they're using.

63
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:55,682
It comes into the load balancer.

64
00:04:55,682 --> 00:05:00,005
The algorithm is going to
pull that pair out,

65
00:05:00,005 --> 00:05:06,076
run a hash function on it, and
then use that hash function.

66
00:05:06,076 --> 00:05:09,540
What's not really shown here is,
it'll select from the set and

67
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:11,478
that will choose which server.

68
00:05:11,478 --> 00:05:14,350
All right, so we're just hashing on that.

69
00:05:14,350 --> 00:05:19,620
Now this is stateless, which is one
of the kind of main benefits of it.

70
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:24,865
And this means that flow affinity is
taken care of by the hash function.

71
00:05:24,865 --> 00:05:29,137
So whenever we have
the exact same session,

72
00:05:29,137 --> 00:05:36,652
we'll always have the same source IP and
source port coming from the client.

73
00:05:36,652 --> 00:05:41,387
So flow affinity doesn't need
to be tracked, it just happens.

74
00:05:43,826 --> 00:05:47,234
A next algorithm would be round robin.

75
00:05:47,234 --> 00:05:53,820
So here a server selection iterates
through each server in order.

76
00:05:53,820 --> 00:05:57,692
So the first connection that comes
through, it would choose server one.

77
00:05:57,692 --> 00:06:00,576
Next connection that comes
through would choose server two.

78
00:06:00,576 --> 00:06:04,644
Next connection it would choose server
three, and then it would go back, and

79
00:06:04,644 --> 00:06:08,110
then the next connection would
choose server one again.

80
00:06:08,110 --> 00:06:14,370
And this ensures a balanced load, assuming
each request is roughly the same load.

81
00:06:14,370 --> 00:06:21,017
And in this case now, the load balancer
needs to keep state for flow affinity,

82
00:06:21,017 --> 00:06:27,460
it needs to know when there's a packet
that came in, if it looks in a table and

83
00:06:27,460 --> 00:06:33,770
says this particular flow was already
chosen to go to server three.

84
00:06:33,770 --> 00:06:39,425
So the algorithm is basically start
at zero, select from the set zero,

85
00:06:39,425 --> 00:06:45,364
that's the server that you're going to go,
and then just increment next and

86
00:06:45,364 --> 00:06:49,376
mod it by the set length
to be able to wrap around.

87
00:06:49,376 --> 00:06:53,744
And then each time this is called,
it'll change,

88
00:06:53,744 --> 00:06:56,904
it'll pick the next one in the list.

89
00:06:56,904 --> 00:06:58,438
So that's round robin.

90
00:06:58,438 --> 00:07:02,442
And a third one we'll mention is
something called least connections.

91
00:07:02,442 --> 00:07:06,610
And this adds a little bit of state so
that's why we're mentioning it.

92
00:07:06,610 --> 00:07:11,136
So, here it'll select a server
based on which server has

93
00:07:11,136 --> 00:07:14,870
the least number of active connections.

94
00:07:14,870 --> 00:07:19,726
So the load balancer here will
track how many open connections.

95
00:07:19,726 --> 00:07:25,018
So, it's keeping a table for
the flow affinity,

96
00:07:25,018 --> 00:07:29,433
and so it needs to keep that state anyway.

97
00:07:29,433 --> 00:07:33,660
So, it can count how many
active connections there are.

98
00:07:33,660 --> 00:07:39,155
So this takes into account that some
requests may be longer in duration,

99
00:07:39,155 --> 00:07:44,134
but assumes each connection imposes
a similar load on a server.

100
00:07:44,134 --> 00:07:48,469
So maybe a client opens up one connection,
TCP connection,

101
00:07:48,469 --> 00:07:53,970
but then is able to send multiple
requests over that connection.

102
00:07:53,970 --> 00:07:59,369
And so if we end up with a lot of
connections on the same server because

103
00:07:59,369 --> 00:08:04,127
of round robin,
that would overload a particular server.

104
00:08:04,127 --> 00:08:06,568
So that kind of takes this into account.

105
00:08:06,568 --> 00:08:11,714
And so here we would look up which has
the minimum active connections and

106
00:08:11,714 --> 00:08:15,143
then use that to choose the server,
all right,

107
00:08:15,143 --> 00:08:19,720
and then kind of keep track of
the number of connections in each.

108
00:08:21,290 --> 00:08:24,972
All right, so now let's step back.

109
00:08:24,972 --> 00:08:30,510
And then what we've been talking
about is a layer 4 load balancer.

110
00:08:30,510 --> 00:08:32,975
Well, we really haven't distinguished, but

111
00:08:32,975 --> 00:08:35,936
we're going to imply that
we're talking about layer 4.

112
00:08:35,936 --> 00:08:41,490
So,let's talk about the difference between
a layer 4 and a layer 7 load balancer.

113
00:08:41,490 --> 00:08:47,330
So layer 4 only considers the network and
transport layer headers.

114
00:08:47,330 --> 00:08:51,270
This is also called
a network load balancer.

115
00:08:51,270 --> 00:08:56,913
Layer 7 load balancer also
considers the application,

116
00:08:56,913 --> 00:09:00,222
for example HTTP, its header.

117
00:09:00,222 --> 00:09:03,246
So this is also called a web proxy.

118
00:09:03,246 --> 00:09:08,218
So for example,
we might have some website and

119
00:09:08,218 --> 00:09:13,571
maybe for the page sign up
we want to go to server 1,

120
00:09:13,571 --> 00:09:19,392
but for the page purchase
we can go to server 2 or 3.

121
00:09:19,392 --> 00:09:24,858
So the layer 7 load balancer
can allow you to route

122
00:09:24,858 --> 00:09:31,900
different kind of requests to
different servers based on that.

123
00:09:35,250 --> 00:09:41,332
It's not either or,
a lot of the times both are used together.

124
00:09:41,332 --> 00:09:46,795
So there was a nice presentation by
Facebook about some of their internal

125
00:09:46,795 --> 00:09:51,730
architecture and they were talking
about load balancing there.

126
00:09:52,950 --> 00:09:57,602
And interestingly enough,
they're using IPVS, and

127
00:09:57,602 --> 00:10:02,570
that's exactly what we'll
talk about next time.

128
00:10:02,570 --> 00:10:05,628
So they have different layers
of load balancing here.

129
00:10:05,628 --> 00:10:11,506
So they talk about DNS, so
they can load balance requests and

130
00:10:11,506 --> 00:10:17,744
direct them to the given data center
that they want them to go to.

131
00:10:17,744 --> 00:10:22,702
Once they get to the data center,
now they're using a network load balancer

132
00:10:22,702 --> 00:10:25,636
to load balance, and
this is kind of quick.

133
00:10:25,636 --> 00:10:30,610
It's a lot simpler to load
balance between a layer 7 proxy,

134
00:10:30,610 --> 00:10:35,584
which then they want this kind of
scale out architecture for,

135
00:10:35,584 --> 00:10:41,590
which then will load balance between
their kind of actual service.

136
00:10:41,590 --> 00:10:46,452
So I thought that was an interesting feel
free to check out this presentation.

137
00:10:46,452 --> 00:10:50,328
They have both the paper as
well as the presentation there.

138
00:10:50,328 --> 00:10:53,090
And this is a screen grab
from that presentation.

139
00:10:54,390 --> 00:11:00,810
And with that we'll end here, and
next time we're going to go into IPVS,

140
00:11:00,810 --> 00:11:05,318
which is the Linux framework for
load balancing.

141
00:11:05,318 --> 00:11:08,202
All right, until then.